  <PipelineDiagram class="not-prose my-8" />

  <h2>Local storage happens first</h2>
  <p>
    A span is written to mcpd's own store before anything is forwarded, and the
    forward runs on its own task afterwards. Two consequences, both deliberate.
  </p>
  <p>
    A slow destination never adds its latency to the request that produced the
    span. Your agent does not wait on someone else's collector.
  </p>
  <p>
    A destination that is down never turns into a rejected ingest. mcpd keeps
    working and retries the forward on its own task. The alternative, where
    another company's outage means your telemetry is not kept at all, is the
    exact failure this design exists to avoid.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each batch gets three attempts. A 429, a 5xx or a failed connection is
    retried after 250 ms, and the wait doubles after each attempt. A
    <code>Retry-After</code> given in seconds replaces the wait, up to 30
    seconds. Any other 4xx is final on the first answer, because the batch will
    stay malformed for that destination. When the daemon is stopping, it makes
    the remaining attempts at once. A batch that is refused for good, or is
    still undelivered after its last attempt, is logged, and its spans are added
    to <code>mcpd_forward_dropped_spans_total</code>, so you can see how much of
    the copy is missing.
  </p>
  <p>
    A destination that accepts a connection and never answers cannot take the
    daemon's memory either. At most 64 ingest requests' copies wait on the
    destination at once. While that many are waiting, a new copy is dropped at
    once and counted in <code>mcpd_forward_dropped_spans_total</code>, and the
    local write is unaffected.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is no durable outbound queue. A batch waits in memory while it is
    retried, and when the forwarding queue is full, a new batch is dropped and
    its spans are counted in the same metric. A durable queue is a different
    product, and a silent one that falls hours behind is worse than a visible
    gap. If you need guaranteed delivery, put a real collector between mcpd and
    the destination and let it do the job it was built for.
  </p>

  <h2>Redaction applies to what leaves</h2>
  <p>
    Forwarding happens after the intake pipeline, which redacts in place before
    anything is written. Whatever mcpd was told to hide never reaches the next
    hop either. A rule that protected your storage but not your egress would not
    be a privacy control.
  </p>

  <h2>What gets forwarded</h2>
  <p>
    Spans. Logs, metrics, scores, feedback and aspect notes stay in mcpd,
    because the destinations people forward to are tracing backends and would
    drop the rest or mangle it into attributes.
  </p>
  <p>
    Spans are grouped by service into <code>resourceSpans</code>, the shape a
    receiver expects, rather than one resource per span, which is valid and
    makes every downstream regroup it.
  </p>

  <h2>mcpd forwarding to mcpd</h2>
  <p>
    Because both ends speak the same protocol, a local mcpd can forward to a
    shared one. Developers keep a fast store on their own machine with full
    payloads, and the team's instance gets the same runs with its own retention
    and its own redaction rules applied on arrival.
  </p>

